        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The objective of this test was to aid in the proper assessment of hemodynamic parameters, vascular responses, and cardiac size and function. Investigators hypothesized that bed rest changes in cardiac dimensions and function will be similar to those seen after space flight.  They expect to see changes in cardiac function which is similar to those seen pre-bedrest.
<div>
APPROACH:<br>
Hemodynamic assessment was accomplished by the use of continuous, pulsed wave, and color flow Doppler.  All four cardiac valves were evaluated for regurgitation.  A critical factor on cardiac function is the relaxation component or diastolic function.  To address the diastolic function, several traditional echocardiographic measurements and indices were used in addition to more investigational measures. The echocardiography was used to give views necessary to image all valves and chambers of the heart.  Electrodes were placed on the subject and ultrasound measurements were made of the heart.
</div><div>
RESULTS:<br>
Results from the Artificial Gravity Study indicate that artificial gravity can mitigate some aspects of bed rest-induced cardiovascular deconditioning, including orthostatic intolerance and aerobic power. Mechanisms of improvement were not cardiac-mediated, but likely through improved sympathetic responsiveness to orthostatic stress. More information can be found in the publications cited.<end></end></div>
        </div>

